/* =====================================================================
   MKT101 study notes
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------
   One entry per paper. A paper with notes gets a "Study notes" tab.
   Definitions follow Armstrong & Kotler, Marketing: An Introduction,
   15th Global Edition. Plain, short sentences for Level 100 students.
   Never put real exam questions here.

   Paper entry: {intro, how:[[step, text]], answer:{h, p, dia, l}, topics:{n:{...}}}
   Topic entry:
     sum:      one-line summary for the topic list
     min:      "In one minute" points: the big picture
     lo:       what students must be able to do
     exam:     what the exam asks (general)
     ideas:    [{h, p (text or list of paragraphs), l (list: text or [term, text]),
                 dia (diagram, see diagramHTML in index.html), more, eg, tip}]
     terms:    [[term, meaning]] for the flip cards
     mistakes: common mistakes
     worked:   {q, plan:[...], model:[[tag, paragraph]]} a practice question with a model answer
     check:    [[question, answer]] with hidden answers
     prompts:  three study prompts to copy into Gemini
   To add notes for the Final, add a final:{...} entry in the same shape.
   ===================================================================== */

const NOTES={
midterm:{
 intro:"Everything you need for the Midterm, in plain English. Each topic has short lessons, diagrams, flip cards, a worked answer, check questions and practice. Start with Topic 1 and go in order.",
 how:[
  ["Read the one-minute summary","Get the big picture of the topic first."],
  ["Work through the key ideas","Read one card at a time. Study each diagram until you can draw it."],
  ["Test yourself","Flip the key term cards, then answer the check questions."],
  ["Practise","Do the 10-question topic quiz. Then write one Section B answer on paper."]
 ],
 answer:{h:"How to write a 5-mark answer",p:"Section B answers are short, but each mark needs a clear point. Use these four moves in almost every answer.",
  dia:{type:"flow",title:"Four moves for full marks",steps:[["Define","Give the textbook meaning in one sentence."],["Explain","Say how it works or why it matters."],["Apply","Use the business in the question. Use its name."],["Example","Give a short, real-looking Maldivian example."]]},
  l:[["Count the marks","5 marks means about 5 clear points. Plan them before you write."],["Read the command word","'Explain' needs reasons. 'Identify' needs only names. 'Describe' needs the main features. 'Recommend' needs a choice and a reason."],["List questions","If the question says 'four', write four separate short paragraphs, one for each."],["Time","Spend about 7 minutes on each answer. Half a page is enough."]]},
 topics:{

 /* ===================== TOPIC 1 ===================== */
 1:{sum:"What marketing is, needs, wants and demands, the five marketing orientations, and building customer relationships.",
  min:["Marketing is about creating value for customers so they give value back: sales, loyalty and good reviews.","It starts with customer needs, wants and demands.","Companies can think about marketing in five ways. The marketing concept and the societal marketing concept put the customer first.","Good marketing builds long-term relationships, because loyal customers are worth much more over time."],
  lo:"Explain the core ideas of marketing and how companies create value for customers and capture value in return.",
  exam:["Section A: define key terms, and spot an orientation or idea in a short business story.","Section B: explain a core idea with an example, or show how a local business could use it to win and keep customers."],
  ideas:[
   {h:"What is marketing?",p:["Many people think marketing is just selling and advertising. That is only a small part of it.","The textbook defines marketing as the process by which companies engage customers, build strong customer relationships and create customer value, in order to capture value from customers in return.","Put simply: first you give customers something of value. Then they give value back to you."],
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"A simple model of the marketing process",steps:[["Understand the market","Learn customer needs and wants."],["Design a strategy","Choose customers and how to serve them."],["Build a programme","Use the 4Ps to deliver value."],["Build relationships","Create value and delight customers."],["Capture value","Get sales, profit and loyalty back."]]},
    more:"The first four steps create value for customers. Only the last step captures value from them. Marketing is mostly about giving first.",
    eg:"A guesthouse on Maafushi learns what divers want, offers early boat trips and gear storage, treats guests well, and gets repeat bookings and five-star reviews in return."},
   {h:"Needs, wants and demands",p:"These three ideas build on each other. Learn them as a ladder.",
    l:[["Needs","States of felt deprivation. They include physical needs (food, clothing, safety), social needs (belonging, love) and individual needs (knowledge, self-expression). Marketers do not create needs. They are a basic part of being human."],["Wants","The form a need takes when it is shaped by culture and individual personality. A hungry person in Malé may want mas huni and roshi. A hungry person in Italy may want pasta."],["Demands","Wants that are backed by buying power. When you have the money to buy what you want, the want becomes a demand."]],
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"From need to demand",steps:[["Need","I am hungry."],["Want","I want a tuna sandwich from my favourite café."],["Demand","I have MVR 50, so I buy it."]]},
    tip:"Always show the link: a need becomes a want through culture and personality, and a want becomes a demand when there is buying power."},
   {h:"Market offerings and marketing myopia",p:["A market offering is some mix of products, services, information or experiences offered to a market to satisfy a need or want. It is more than a physical product. A resort offers rooms, but also service, information about the reefs and an experience to remember.","Marketing myopia is the mistake of paying more attention to the products you make than to the benefits and experiences they give. Myopia means short-sightedness. A myopic company forgets why customers really buy."],
    eg:"A boat builder that only talks about its engines, and forgets that fishermen are buying a safe trip and a good catch, has marketing myopia."},
   {h:"Customer value, satisfaction, exchange and markets",l:[["Customer value and satisfaction","Customers choose the offer that they believe gives the most value. Satisfaction depends on whether the offer meets their expectations. More on this below."],["Exchange","The act of getting something you want from someone by offering something in return. Marketing is all about exchange relationships."],["Market","The set of all actual and possible buyers of a product or service. A market includes people who have not bought yet."]]},
   {h:"The five marketing management orientations",p:"An orientation is a way of thinking. It shows what a company puts first when it plans its marketing. The textbook gives five.",
    l:[["Production concept","Consumers prefer products that are available and very cheap. The company works to make and distribute efficiently. Risk: it may lose sight of what customers want."],["Product concept","Consumers prefer products with the most quality, performance and new features. The company keeps improving the product. Risk: marketing myopia."],["Selling concept","Consumers will not buy enough unless the company sells and promotes hard. Often used for unsought goods. Risk: it sells what it makes, not what the market wants, and does not build long-term relationships."],["Marketing concept","The company reaches its goals by knowing the needs and wants of target markets and delivering satisfaction better than competitors. It is customer-centred: 'sense and respond'."],["Societal marketing concept","Marketing should also protect or improve the long-term welfare of consumers and society. It balances three things: company profits, consumer wants and society's interests."]],
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"The selling concept and the marketing concept",cols:[["Selling concept (inside out)",["Starts at the factory","Focus: existing products","Means: selling and promoting","Ends: profit through sales volume"]],["Marketing concept (outside in)",["Starts with the market","Focus: customer needs","Means: integrated marketing","Ends: profit through customer satisfaction"]]]},
    eg:"A juice brand that cuts sugar and uses recyclable bottles, even though it costs more, follows the societal marketing concept.",
    tip:"In a story question, look for clues. 'Cheap and everywhere' means production. 'Best quality' means product. 'Push hard to sell' means selling. 'Find out what customers need' means marketing. 'Good for society too' means societal."},
   {h:"Customer value and customer satisfaction",p:["Customer-perceived value is the customer's judgement of the difference between all the benefits and all the costs of an offer, compared with competitors' offers. Customers do not judge value exactly. They act on what they believe.","Customer satisfaction depends on how the product's perceived performance compares with what the buyer expected."],
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Performance compared with expectations",cols:[["Below expectations",["The customer is dissatisfied.","They may complain and not return."]],["Matches expectations",["The customer is satisfied.","They may come back."]],["Above expectations",["The customer is delighted.","They return and tell others."]]]},
    more:"Smart companies try to delight customers. But they promise only what they can deliver, so expectations stay realistic.",
    eg:"A speedboat transfer that is on time and clean satisfies guests. If the crew also helps with bags and gives cold towels and water, guests are delighted."},
   {h:"Building customer relationships",p:"Customer relationship management (CRM) is the overall process of building and keeping profitable customer relationships by delivering superior customer value and satisfaction. Today it also includes new ways to connect.",
    l:[["Relationship building tools","Basic relationships for many low-margin customers (a thank-you SMS). Full partnerships for a few high-value customers (a personal account manager). Loyalty and retention programmes, such as points cards."],["Customer-engagement marketing","Making the brand a real part of customers' conversations, experiences and lives, often through social media and online communities."],["Consumer-generated marketing","Brand content that customers create themselves, such as reviews, photos, videos and posts."],["Partner relationship management","Working closely with other departments and outside partners, such as suppliers and distributors, to bring more value to customers."]],
    eg:"Guests at a dive centre post their dive videos and tag the centre on Instagram. That is consumer-generated marketing."},
   {h:"Capturing value from customers",p:"When a company creates value for customers, it gets value back. Three measures show this value.",
    l:[["Customer lifetime value","The value of the whole stream of purchases a customer makes over a lifetime of buying from the company."],["Share of customer","The portion of a customer's buying in a product category that the company gets. A café wants more of your total coffee spending."],["Customer equity","The total combined customer lifetime values of all the company's current and possible customers. It is a good measure of a company's future value."]],
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"How loyalty grows value",steps:[["Delight the customer","Give more than they expect."],["Loyalty and repeat buying","They come back again and again."],["Higher lifetime value","Each customer is worth more."],["Higher customer equity","The whole business is worth more."]]},
    eg:"A diver who visits the same dive centre every year for ten years spends far more than a one-time visitor. Keeping that diver happy is very valuable."}
  ],
  terms:[["Marketing","Engaging customers, building strong relationships and creating customer value, to capture value from customers in return."],["Needs","States of felt deprivation."],["Wants","The form needs take when shaped by culture and personality."],["Demands","Wants backed by buying power."],["Market offering","A mix of products, services, information or experiences offered to satisfy a need or want."],["Marketing myopia","Paying more attention to products than to the benefits and experiences they give."],["Exchange","Getting something you want by offering something in return."],["Market","All the actual and possible buyers of a product or service."],["Societal marketing concept","Balancing company profits, consumer wants and society's long-term interests."],["Customer-perceived value","The customer's judgement of benefits and costs compared with competitors' offers."],["Customer satisfaction","How well perceived performance matches the buyer's expectations."],["CRM","Building and keeping profitable customer relationships by delivering superior value and satisfaction."],["Customer lifetime value","The value of all the purchases a customer makes over a lifetime."],["Share of customer","The portion of a customer's category buying that the company gets."],["Customer equity","The total lifetime values of all the company's customers."]],
  mistakes:["Saying marketing is 'selling and advertising'. Selling is only one small part.","Mixing up needs and wants. Hunger is a need. A pizza is a want.","Forgetting buying power when you explain demand.","Mixing up the selling concept and the marketing concept. Selling starts with the product. Marketing starts with the customer.","Mixing up customer lifetime value (one customer) and customer equity (all customers)."],
  worked:{q:"A bakery in Hulhumalé has many customers who buy once and never return. Explain how the bakery could capture more value from its customers. Use the ideas of customer lifetime value and share of customer.",
   plan:["Define customer lifetime value.","Explain why keeping customers increases it, using the bakery.","Define share of customer.","Explain one way the bakery can win more of each customer's spending.","Give a short closing link to customer equity."],
   model:[["Define","Customer lifetime value is the value of the whole stream of purchases a customer makes over a lifetime of buying from a company."],["Apply","A customer who buys bread twice a week for five years is worth far more to the bakery than a one-time visitor. So the bakery should focus on keeping customers, not only on finding new ones. A simple loyalty card, where every tenth loaf is free, would give customers a reason to return."],["Define","Share of customer is the portion of a customer's buying in a category that the company gets."],["Apply","The bakery could win more of each customer's spending by adding products they already buy elsewhere, such as birthday cakes and short eats, and by offering a breakfast bundle of bread, eggs and tea."],["Link","Higher lifetime value and a bigger share of customer together raise the bakery's customer equity, which is the total value of all its customers."]]},
  check:[
   ["What is the difference between a want and a demand?","A demand is a want backed by buying power. You can want something you cannot pay for, but it only becomes demand when you can buy it."],
   ["A phone shop pushes extended warranties on every customer to meet sales targets. Which orientation is this?","The selling concept. The shop focuses on making the sale, not on what the customer needs."],
   ["A guest expected a good stay and got an amazing one. Is the guest satisfied or delighted?","Delighted. Performance went beyond expectations."],
   ["What are the three things the societal marketing concept balances?","Company profits, consumer wants and society's long-term interests."],
   ["What is customer equity?","The total combined customer lifetime values of all the company's current and possible customers."]
  ],
  prompts:[
   "I am a Level 100 marketing student in the Maldives. Explain the difference between needs, wants and demands in simple English. Use two examples from Maldivian life. Then ask me three short questions, one at a time, to check that I understand.",
   "Explain the five marketing management orientations (production, product, selling, marketing and societal marketing) in simple English. For each one, give an example of a Maldivian business that might follow it. Make a short table at the end.",
   "Act as my marketing tutor. Give me a short story about a Maldivian guesthouse that wants more repeat guests. Ask me how it could use customer relationship management, customer lifetime value and customer engagement. Give me feedback on my answer."
  ]},

 /* ===================== TOPIC 2 ===================== */
 2:{sum:"Strategic planning, the mission, the BCG matrix, growth strategies, the value chain and the marketing mix.",
  min:["Strategic planning decides where the whole company is going. Marketing helps it get there.","The planning steps are: mission, objectives, business portfolio, then functional plans.","The BCG matrix and the product/market expansion grid help companies decide which businesses to grow.","Marketing works with partners and uses the 4Ps. SWOT, planning, implementation and control keep the plan on track."],
  lo:"Explain how a company plans its strategy, and how marketing fits into the plan with partners and the marketing mix.",
  exam:["Section A: define planning terms, and place a business in the BCG matrix or the growth grid.","Section B: explain a planning tool, or apply it to a Maldivian company's options."],
  ideas:[
   {h:"Strategic planning",p:"Strategic planning is the process of developing and keeping a strategic fit between the organisation's goals and abilities and its changing marketing opportunities. It is the base for all other planning in the company. It has four steps.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"Steps in strategic planning",steps:[["Define the mission","Why does the company exist?"],["Set objectives and goals","What must it achieve?"],["Design the business portfolio","Which businesses and products?"],["Plan functional strategies","Marketing and other departments' plans."]]},
    more:"The first three steps happen at company level. The last step happens in each department, such as marketing, finance and operations."},
   {h:"The mission statement",p:["A mission statement is a statement of the organisation's purpose: what it wants to achieve in the larger environment.","A good mission is market-oriented. It describes the business in terms of meeting basic customer needs, not in terms of its products or technology. Products change over time. Customer needs last."],
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Product-oriented and market-oriented missions",cols:[["Product-oriented",["'We run ferries.'","'We sell phones.'","'We make tuna cans.'"]],["Market-oriented",["'We connect island families safely and on time.'","'We help people stay in touch with the people they love.'","'We bring healthy, easy meals to busy families.'"]]]},
    more:"A good mission should also be meaningful, specific, realistic and motivating. Missions then turn into clear objectives, such as 'increase market share by 10% this year'.",
    tip:"If a question asks what a good mission is based on, the answer is customer needs."},
   {h:"The business portfolio and the BCG matrix",p:["The business portfolio is the collection of businesses and products that make up the company. Each business is a strategic business unit (SBU).","Portfolio analysis helps managers decide which businesses to invest in. The best-known tool is the Boston Consulting Group (BCG) growth-share matrix. It looks at two things: how fast the market is growing, and how big the SBU's market share is compared with its largest competitor."],
    dia:{type:"grid",title:"BCG growth-share matrix",yl:"Market growth rate",xl:"Relative market share",y:["High","Low"],x:["High","Low"],cells:[["Star","High share, high growth. Needs heavy investment to keep growing. Becomes a cash cow later."],["Question mark","Low share, high growth. Needs lots of cash. Build it into a star, or drop it."],["Cash cow","High share, low growth. Brings in cash to pay for other businesses."],["Dog","Low share, low growth. Makes little money. Often dropped."]]},
    l:[["Build","Invest to increase share. Often for question marks."],["Hold","Invest just enough to keep share. Often for strong cash cows."],["Harvest","Take short-term cash, whatever happens later. Often for weak cash cows or dogs."],["Divest","Sell or close the business, so money can go elsewhere."]],
    eg:"A resort group's famous villa brand in a market that has stopped growing is a cash cow. Its new eco-lodge brand in a fast-growing market, with only a small share so far, is a question mark."},
   {h:"Growth: the product/market expansion grid",p:"This grid shows four ways a company can grow. Ask two questions: are the products current or new? Are the markets current or new?",
    dia:{type:"grid",title:"Product/market expansion grid",yl:"Markets",xl:"Products",y:["Existing","New"],x:["Existing","New"],cells:[["Market penetration","Sell more of current products to current customers."],["Product development","Offer new or changed products to current customers."],["Market development","Take current products to new markets."],["Diversification","Start or buy businesses with new products in new markets."]]},
    eg:"A Malé bakery that adds sugar-free cakes for its regular customers is using product development. If it opens a branch in Addu with the same menu, that is market development.",
    tip:"Diversification is the riskiest, because both the product and the market are new."},
   {h:"Downsizing",p:"Companies also need to shrink sometimes. Downsizing means reducing the business portfolio by dropping products or businesses that are no longer profitable or that no longer fit the company's overall strategy."},
   {h:"Partnering to build customer relationships",p:"Marketing cannot create customer value alone. It needs partners inside and outside the company.",
    l:[["Value chain","The series of internal departments that carry out value-creating activities to design, produce, market, deliver and support the product. Every department matters."],["Value delivery network","A wider network: the company, its suppliers, its distributors and finally its customers, who partner with each other to improve the performance of the whole system."]],
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"A value delivery network",steps:[["Suppliers","Fish, oil, cans."],["The company","A tuna factory."],["Distributors","Wholesalers and island shops."],["Customers","Families who buy the tuna."]]}},
   {h:"Marketing strategy and the marketing mix",p:["Marketing strategy is the marketing logic by which the company hopes to create customer value and achieve profitable relationships. It has two parts. First, decide which customers to serve: segmentation and targeting. Second, decide how to serve them: differentiation and positioning. (You will study these in Topic 6.)","Then the company builds an integrated marketing mix: the set of tactical tools it blends to get the response it wants from the target market."],
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"The four Ps of the marketing mix",cols:[["Product",["The goods and services offered","Quality, design, features, brand, packaging"]],["Price",["What customers pay","List price, discounts, payment terms"]],["Place",["How the product reaches customers","Channels, locations, stock, delivery"]],["Promotion",["How the company communicates","Advertising, sales promotion, social media, personal selling"]]]},
    eg:"A café decides its menu (product), sets a coffee at MVR 35 (price), opens near MNU and delivers through an app (place), and posts daily specials on Instagram (promotion)."},
   {h:"Managing the marketing effort",p:"Marketing management has four functions. They form a cycle: what you learn from control feeds into the next analysis.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"Managing marketing",steps:[["Analysis","Study the situation, often with SWOT."],["Planning","Choose strategies and write the plan."],["Implementation","Turn plans into actions."],["Control","Measure results and fix problems."]],loop:"Control results feed back into analysis."},
    l:[["Planning and implementation","Planning says what to do and why. Implementation says who does it, where, when and how. A great plan fails if it is not put into action."],["Control","Set goals, measure performance, find the causes of any gap, then take corrective action."]]},
   {h:"SWOT analysis",p:"SWOT analysis is an overall look at the company's strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats. Strengths and weaknesses are inside the company and it can control them. Opportunities and threats are outside the company and it cannot control them.",
    dia:{type:"grid",title:"SWOT analysis",yl:"Where it comes from",xl:"Effect on the company",y:["Internal","External"],x:["Helpful","Harmful"],cells:[["Strengths","Good things inside: skilled staff, strong brand, good location."],["Weaknesses","Problems inside: old equipment, few staff, poor website."],["Opportunities","Good trends outside: more tourists, new flight routes."],["Threats","Bad trends outside: new rivals, higher fuel prices, new taxes."]]},
    tip:"The most common mistake is putting an outside trend under 'weakness'. Ask: can the company control it? If not, it is an opportunity or a threat."},
   {h:"Measuring marketing return",p:"Managers must show that marketing spending pays off. Return on marketing investment (marketing ROI) is the net return from a marketing investment divided by the cost of that investment. It can be measured with brand awareness, sales, market share, and customer measures such as retention and lifetime value."}
  ],
  terms:[["Strategic planning","Developing and keeping a fit between the company's goals and abilities and its changing opportunities."],["Mission statement","A statement of the organisation's purpose."],["Business portfolio","The collection of businesses and products that make up the company."],["Star","High share in a high-growth market."],["Cash cow","High share in a low-growth market."],["Question mark","Low share in a high-growth market."],["Dog","Low share in a low-growth market."],["Market penetration","More sales of current products to current markets."],["Market development","Current products in new markets."],["Product development","New or changed products for current markets."],["Diversification","New products in new markets."],["Downsizing","Dropping products or businesses that no longer pay or fit."],["Value chain","Internal departments that create value."],["Value delivery network","The company, suppliers, distributors and customers working as partners."],["Marketing mix","Product, price, place and promotion."],["SWOT analysis","Strengths, weaknesses, opportunities and threats."],["Marketing ROI","Net return from marketing divided by its cost."]],
  mistakes:["Writing a product-oriented mission ('we sell X') when asked for a good mission.","Swapping the BCG axes. Up and down is market growth. Left and right is market share.","Mixing up market development (new market) and product development (new product).","Putting outside trends under strengths or weaknesses in a SWOT.","Mixing up the value chain (inside the company) and the value delivery network (with outside partners)."],
  worked:{q:"A Maldivian company owns four businesses: a popular bottled water brand in a market that has stopped growing, a new mobile payment app in a fast-growing market with very few users so far, a leading online travel booking site in a fast-growing market, and an old video rental shop with few customers. Use the BCG matrix to classify each business and suggest a strategy for each.",
   plan:["One sentence: what the BCG matrix measures (market growth and relative share).","Take each business in turn. Name its box and give the reason using growth and share.","Add one strategy for each: build, hold, harvest or divest."],
   model:[["Define","The BCG growth-share matrix classifies a company's businesses by market growth rate and relative market share."],["Apply","The bottled water brand has high share in a low-growth market, so it is a cash cow. The company should hold it and use its cash to fund other businesses."],["Apply","The mobile payment app has low share in a fast-growing market, so it is a question mark. The company should build it with investment if it believes it can win, or drop it if not."],["Apply","The online booking site has high share in a fast-growing market, so it is a star. The company should build it to keep its lead until the market slows."],["Apply","The video rental shop has low share in a market with no growth, so it is a dog. The company should harvest it for any last cash, or divest it."]]},
  check:[
   ["What makes a mission statement market-oriented?","It defines the business by the customer needs it meets, not by the products it makes."],
   ["A business has high share in a low-growth market. Where does it sit in the BCG matrix?","It is a cash cow."],
   ["A Maldivian airline starts selling travel insurance to new customers abroad. Which growth strategy is this?","Diversification: a new product in a new market."],
   ["Is 'rising fuel prices' a weakness or a threat for a ferry company? Why?","A threat. It comes from outside the company, and the company cannot control it."],
   ["Name the four marketing management functions.","Analysis, planning, implementation and control."]
  ],
  prompts:[
   "I am a Level 100 marketing student in the Maldives. Explain the BCG growth-share matrix in simple English. Use an imaginary Maldivian company with four businesses and put each one in the matrix. Then quiz me with three questions, one at a time.",
   "Explain the product/market expansion grid in simple English. Give one example of each growth strategy for a small café in Malé. Then give me two new examples and ask me to name the strategy.",
   "Help me practise SWOT analysis. Describe a small guesthouse on a local island in the Maldives in five sentences. Ask me to write one strength, weakness, opportunity and threat. Then check my answers and explain any mistakes."
  ]},

 /* ===================== TOPIC 3 ===================== */
 3:{sum:"The microenvironment, the six macroenvironment forces, publics, and how companies respond to change.",
  min:["The marketing environment is everything outside marketing that affects how well a company serves its customers.","The microenvironment is the actors close to the company: the company itself, suppliers, intermediaries, competitors, publics and customers.","The macroenvironment is six big forces: demographic, economic, natural, technological, political and social, and cultural.","Smart companies watch these forces and act early. Some even try to shape them."],
  lo:"Describe the forces in a company's marketing environment and explain how changes in them affect marketing decisions.",
  exam:["Section A: name the actor or force behind a change, and know the types of publics and markets.","Section B: explain the two environments, or analyse how changes in the environment affect a Maldivian business."],
  ideas:[
   {h:"The marketing environment",p:["The marketing environment includes the actors and forces outside marketing that affect marketing management's ability to build and keep successful relationships with target customers.","It has two layers. The microenvironment is close to the company. The macroenvironment is the bigger forces in society that affect everyone in the microenvironment."],
    dia:{type:"rings",title:"Layers of the marketing environment",rings:[["Macroenvironment","Demographic, economic, natural, technological, political and social, cultural forces."],["Microenvironment","Suppliers, marketing intermediaries, competitors, publics, customers."],["The company","Top management, finance, operations, HR, marketing."]]},
    tip:"A quick test: if you can name the person or business, it is probably micro. If it is a trend in society, it is macro."},
   {h:"The microenvironment: six actors",p:"The microenvironment consists of the actors close to the company that affect its ability to engage and serve its customers.",
    l:[["The company","All the other departments: top management, finance, IT, purchasing, operations, HR. Marketing must work with all of them."],["Suppliers","Businesses that provide the resources the company needs to make its goods and services. Shortages, delays and price rises hurt sales and customer satisfaction."],["Marketing intermediaries","Businesses that help the company promote, sell and distribute its products to final buyers: resellers (wholesalers, shops), physical distribution firms (shipping, storage), marketing services agencies (ad agencies, research firms) and financial intermediaries (banks, insurers)."],["Competitors","To succeed, a company must give more customer value and satisfaction than its competitors."],["Publics","Any group that has an actual or possible interest in, or impact on, the organisation's ability to achieve its goals."],["Customers","The most important actors. There are five types of customer markets: consumer, business, reseller, government and international."]],
    eg:"For a café in Malé: the milk importer is a supplier, the food delivery app is an intermediary, the café across the road is a competitor, and office workers nearby are customers."},
   {h:"Seven types of publics",p:"A public is any group that has an actual or possible interest in, or impact on, an organisation's ability to achieve its goals.",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Publics around a resort",cols:[["Financial","Banks and investors. Affect access to money."],["Media","News sites, TV, bloggers. Carry news and opinion."],["Government","Ministries and councils. Set and check rules."],["Citizen-action","Consumer and environmental groups. Question decisions."],["Internal","Staff, managers, the board. Shape the company from inside."],["Local","Neighbours and community groups nearby."],["General","The wider public's image of the company."]].map(([h,x])=>[h,[x]])}},
   {h:"Five types of customer markets",l:[["Consumer markets","Individuals and households who buy for personal use."],["Business markets","Organisations that buy to process further or to use in their production."],["Reseller markets","Businesses that buy to resell at a profit."],["Government markets","Government agencies that buy to produce public services."],["International markets","Buyers in other countries."]],
    eg:"A tuna company sells to families (consumer), to resorts for their kitchens (business), to island shops (reseller), to hospitals run by the state (government), and abroad (international)."},
   {h:"The macroenvironment: six forces",p:"The macroenvironment is the larger societal forces that affect the whole microenvironment. Learn the six forces with the memory aid DENTPC: Demographic, Economic, Natural, Technological, Political and social, Cultural.",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"The six macroenvironment forces",cols:[["Demographic","Population size, age, location, gender, family, jobs."],["Economic","Income, prices, savings, how people spend."],["Natural","Natural resources and the effect of marketing on nature."],["Technological","New technologies that create new products and markets."],["Political and social","Laws, government agencies, pressure groups."],["Cultural","Values, beliefs and ways of life."]].map(([h,x])=>[h,[x]])}},
   {h:"Demographic environment",p:"Demography is the study of human populations in terms of size, density, location, age, gender, race, occupation and other statistics. Marketers care because people make up markets.",
    l:["Changing age structure, such as more young adults or more older people.","Changing family types, such as smaller families or more people living alone.","People moving from place to place.","Better education and more white-collar jobs.","Growing diversity."],
    eg:"Many young families are moving from Malé to Hulhumalé. Shops, schools and cafés there have a growing market."},
   {h:"Economic environment",p:"The economic environment consists of economic factors that affect consumer buying power and spending patterns. Marketers watch income, the cost of living, interest rates and how people divide their spending.",
    eg:"When rent in Malé goes up, families have less left to spend on eating out. Restaurants may offer cheaper set meals.",
    tip:"If a story is about money, prices, income or spending, the force is economic."},
   {h:"Natural environment",p:"The natural environment covers the physical environment and the natural resources that marketers need as inputs, or that are affected by marketing activities. Trends include shortages of raw materials, more pollution, and more government action on sustainability.",
    eg:"Coral bleaching and beach erosion hurt resorts. Many Maldivian businesses now cut single-use plastic and use solar power."},
   {h:"Technological environment",p:"The technological environment is the forces that create new technologies, new products and new market opportunities. It changes very fast. New technology can create new markets and destroy old ones.",
    eg:"Online booking apps, mobile wallets and food delivery apps have changed how Maldivians buy."},
   {h:"Political and social environment",p:"The political environment consists of laws, government agencies and pressure groups that influence or limit organisations and people. Laws protect companies from each other, protect consumers from unfair practices, and protect society. Companies are also expected to act ethically and be socially responsible.",
    eg:"A new tax, a ban on some plastics, or a rule about consumer warranties are all political changes."},
   {h:"Cultural environment",p:["The cultural environment is made up of institutions and other forces that affect a society's basic values, perceptions, preferences and behaviours.","Core beliefs and values are passed from parents to children and are strengthened by schools, religion and government. They are very hard to change. Secondary beliefs and values change more easily, so marketers have more chance to influence them."],
    l:["People's views of themselves, others, organisations, society, nature and the universe all shift slowly over time."],
    eg:"More young Maldivians now choose healthy food and gym workouts. That is a shift in cultural values."},
   {h:"Responding to the marketing environment",p:"Companies can respond in two ways.",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Two ways to respond",cols:[["Reactive",["Sees the environment as something it cannot control","Watches and adapts to changes"]],["Proactive",["Takes action to shape the publics and forces in its environment","Lobbies, runs campaigns, builds good relations, uses PR"]]]},
    eg:"Guesthouse owners who meet the government to ask for fairer licence fees are being proactive."}
  ],
  terms:[["Marketing environment","Actors and forces outside marketing that affect its ability to serve target customers."],["Microenvironment","Actors close to the company that affect its ability to serve customers."],["Macroenvironment","Larger societal forces that affect the whole microenvironment."],["Marketing intermediaries","Firms that help promote, sell and distribute products to final buyers."],["Public","Any group with an interest in, or impact on, the organisation's ability to reach its goals."],["Demography","The study of human populations: size, location, age, gender, occupation and more."],["Economic environment","Factors that affect buying power and spending patterns."],["Natural environment","Natural resources needed by marketers or affected by marketing."],["Technological environment","Forces that create new technologies, products and opportunities."],["Political environment","Laws, government agencies and pressure groups."],["Cultural environment","Forces that affect a society's basic values, perceptions and behaviours."],["Core beliefs","Values passed from parents to children; very hard to change."],["Reseller market","Businesses that buy to resell at a profit."]],
  mistakes:["Putting competitors or suppliers in the macroenvironment. They are micro.","Calling a change in income 'demographic'. Money is economic.","Mixing up natural (resources, pollution, climate) and cultural (values and beliefs).","Listing forces without explaining how the change affects the business. Always add the 'so what' for the company."],
  worked:{q:"Explain how changes in the technological environment can affect a travel agency in Malé. Give two changes and how the agency should respond.",
   plan:["Define the technological environment.","Change 1: name it, explain the effect, give a response.","Change 2: name it, explain the effect, give a response.","Finish with one sentence: technology brings both threats and opportunities."],
   model:[["Define","The technological environment is the forces that create new technologies, new products and new market opportunities."],["Apply","First, more travellers now book flights and hotels themselves on websites and apps. This is a threat, because customers may skip the agency. The agency should build its own simple booking app or WhatsApp service and focus on things apps cannot do well, such as complex island-hopping trips."],["Apply","Second, social media lets the agency reach customers cheaply. Short videos on TikTok and Instagram can show trips and attract young travellers. The agency should post regular content and reply to messages quickly."],["Link","So new technology can take customers away, but it also gives the agency new ways to create value and reach more people."]]},
  check:[
   ["What is the main difference between the microenvironment and the macroenvironment?","The microenvironment is the actors close to the company. The macroenvironment is the larger societal forces that affect all of them."],
   ["A bank refuses a loan to a guesthouse. Which type of public is the bank?","A financial public."],
   ["Name the six macroenvironment forces.","Demographic, economic, natural, technological, political and social, and cultural."],
   ["More tourists now pay with mobile wallets. Which force is this?","The technological environment."],
   ["Island shops buy from Malé wholesalers to sell again. Which customer market are they?","The reseller market."]
  ],
  prompts:[
   "I am a Level 100 marketing student in the Maldives. Explain the six forces of the macroenvironment in simple English, with one Maldivian example for each. Then give me four news-style sentences and ask me to name the force in each one.",
   "Explain the actors in a company's microenvironment using a Maldivian resort as the example. Make a simple table with the actor, who it is for the resort, and how it can help or harm the resort.",
   "Act as my tutor. Describe a Maldivian food company facing two changes in its environment. Ask me to name the force behind each change and suggest a marketing response. Then give me feedback."
  ]},

 /* ===================== TOPIC 4 ===================== */
 4:{sum:"Marketing information systems, primary and secondary data, the research process, research approaches and sampling.",
  min:["Companies need good information to understand customers. This gives them customer insights.","A marketing information system gathers information from three sources: internal databases, marketing intelligence and marketing research.","Research follows clear steps: define the problem, plan, collect, analyse, then report and act.","Researchers choose the type of data, the research approach, the contact method and the sample."],
  lo:"Explain how companies gather and use marketing information, and apply the marketing research process to a real problem.",
  exam:["Section A: tell types of data, research and samples apart, and name the method in a short story.","Section B: explain the parts of a marketing information system or a research idea, or plan research for a Maldivian business."],
  ideas:[
   {h:"Customer insights",p:"Companies have more data than ever, but data alone is not enough. Customer insights are fresh understandings of customers and the marketplace, taken from marketing information. They become the basis for creating customer value and engagement."},
   {h:"The marketing information system (MIS)",p:"A marketing information system (MIS) is the people and procedures used to assess information needs, develop the needed information, and help decision makers use it to create and check customer and market insights.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"How an MIS works",steps:[["Assess information needs","What do managers really need to know?"],["Develop information","From internal databases, marketing intelligence and marketing research."],["Analyse and use","Turn data into insights and decisions."]]},
    l:[["Internal databases","Information from inside the company's own network, such as sales records, bookings, customer details and complaints. Quick and cheap, but may be incomplete or in the wrong form."],["Marketing intelligence","The systematic collection and analysis of publicly available information about consumers, competitors and developments in the market. For example, reading competitors' websites, social media and news."],["Marketing research","The systematic design, collection, analysis and reporting of data about a specific marketing situation."]],
    tip:"Ask: where does the information come from? Inside the company: internal database. Public sources about rivals and trends: intelligence. A study designed for this problem: research."},
   {h:"The marketing research process",p:"The textbook groups research into four steps. Your lecturers often teach the same process as five steps, by splitting 'implement' into collect and analyse. The five-step version is safest to learn for written answers.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"The marketing research process",steps:[["Define the problem and objectives","What exactly do we need to find out?"],["Develop the research plan","What data, which method, which sample?"],["Collect the data","Run the survey, interviews or observation."],["Analyse the data","Find patterns and answers."],["Report and act","Share the findings and use them to decide."]]},
    more:"Defining the problem is often the hardest step. A badly defined problem wastes the whole study.",
    eg:"A guesthouse with falling bookings would first ask 'why are bookings falling, and from which guests?' before collecting any data."},
   {h:"Three types of research objective",p:"The objective decides the kind of research.",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Research objectives",cols:[["Exploratory",["Gathers early information","Helps define the problem and suggest ideas","Used when the problem is unclear"]],["Descriptive",["Describes things","Market size, who buys, customer attitudes","Answers who, what, how many"]],["Causal",["Tests cause and effect","Does X cause Y?","Example: does a lower price raise sales?"]]]}},
   {h:"Primary and secondary data",p:["Secondary data is information that already exists somewhere, collected for another purpose. Primary data is information collected for the specific purpose at hand.","Researchers usually start with secondary data, because it is faster and cheaper. If it is not enough, they collect primary data."],
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Secondary and primary data",cols:[["Secondary data",["Already exists","Faster and cheaper","May be old, not exactly relevant, or not accurate","Example: tourism ministry arrival figures"]],["Primary data",["Collected now, for this problem","Relevant and up to date","Slower and more expensive","Example: your own survey of guests"]]]},
    tip:"Good data, of either kind, must be relevant, accurate, current and unbiased."},
   {h:"Research approaches for primary data",l:[["Observational research","Gathering primary data by watching relevant people, actions and situations. Shows what people really do."],["Ethnographic research","A type of observation where trained observers watch and interact with people in their natural environment, such as at home or at work."],["Survey research","Asking people questions about their knowledge, attitudes, preferences and buying behaviour. The most widely used method. Best for descriptive information."],["Experimental research","Using groups of people, giving them different treatments, controlling other factors, and checking the differences in response. Best for causal information."]],
    eg:"A supermarket watching which shelves shoppers visit is observation. A gym emailing a questionnaire is a survey. A café testing two prices in two similar branches is an experiment."},
   {h:"Contact methods",l:[["Mail and telephone","Older methods. Mail is cheap but slow, with low response. Telephone is quicker but people may hang up."],["Personal interviewing","Individual interviews, or group interviews called focus groups: about 6 to 10 people talk with a trained moderator about a product or issue. Rich information, but small samples."],["Online research","Online surveys, online focus groups, experiments and social media listening. Fast and cheap, but it can be hard to control who answers."]]},
   {h:"Sampling",p:"A sample is a segment of the population chosen to represent the whole population. Researchers make three decisions: who to survey (sampling unit), how many (sample size), and how to choose them (sampling procedure).",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Types of samples",cols:[["Probability samples",["Simple random: everyone has a known and equal chance","Stratified random: split into groups, then random from each group"]],["Non-probability samples",["Convenience: the easiest people to reach","Judgement: people the researcher thinks are good sources"]]]}},
   {h:"Research instruments",p:"The questionnaire is the most common instrument. It can be used in person, by phone or online.",
    l:[["Closed-end questions","Include all the possible answers. Easy to count and compare."],["Open-end questions","Let people answer in their own words. Useful in exploratory research."]],
    more:"Researchers also use mechanical and digital instruments, such as people meters, store scanners and eye-tracking."},
   {h:"Big data and marketing analytics",p:["Big data is the very large and complex data sets created by today's technologies, such as websites, apps, loyalty cards and social media.","Marketing analytics is the analysis tools, technologies and processes that help marketers find meaningful patterns in big data, to gain customer insights and measure marketing performance.","Companies must use customer data ethically and protect privacy."],
    eg:"An airline can use its booking data to find frequent travellers to Colombo and send them a special offer."}
  ],
  terms:[["Customer insights","Fresh understandings of customers and the market, from marketing information."],["Marketing information system","People and procedures to assess needs, develop information and help managers use it."],["Internal databases","Information from the company's own records."],["Marketing intelligence","Publicly available information about consumers, competitors and the market."],["Marketing research","Design, collection, analysis and reporting of data for a specific situation."],["Exploratory research","Early research to define the problem and suggest ideas."],["Descriptive research","Research that describes markets, buyers and attitudes."],["Causal research","Research that tests cause-and-effect relationships."],["Secondary data","Information that already exists, collected for another purpose."],["Primary data","Information collected for the purpose at hand."],["Observational research","Watching relevant people, actions and situations."],["Ethnographic research","Observing people in their natural environment."],["Survey research","Asking people about knowledge, attitudes and behaviour."],["Experimental research","Comparing matched groups given different treatments."],["Focus group","A small group interview led by a moderator."],["Sample","A segment of the population chosen to represent the whole."],["Big data","Very large, complex data sets from modern technology."]],
  mistakes:["Thinking internal company records are primary data. They already exist, so they are secondary.","Mixing up exploratory (unclear problem) and causal (cause and effect).","Calling a focus group a survey. A focus group is a group interview.","Writing the research steps without applying them to the business in the question."],
  worked:{q:"A Maldivian supermarket chain wants to know whether playing calm music in its stores makes shoppers spend more. Explain which type of research objective and which research approach it should use, and how it could do the study.",
   plan:["Name the research objective and explain why it fits.","Name the research approach and explain why it fits.","Describe the study in simple steps, with a fair comparison.","Say what data to collect and one thing to watch out for."],
   model:[["Identify","This is a causal research objective, because the chain wants to know if one thing (music) causes another (more spending)."],["Explain","The best approach is experimental research, which gives matched groups different treatments, controls other factors, and compares the results."],["Apply","The chain could choose two similar stores with similar customers and sales. For four weeks, one store plays calm music and the other plays none. Prices, staff and promotions stay the same in both."],["Apply","It would compare the average amount each shopper spends in the two stores, using its till records."],["Evaluate","It must keep everything else equal. For example, if one store runs a sale during the test, the results will not be fair."]]},
  check:[
   ["A guesthouse uses tourist arrival figures published by the tourism ministry. Is this primary or secondary data?","Secondary data. It already exists and was collected by someone else for another purpose."],
   ["Which research type would you use first when the problem is not clear?","Exploratory research."],
   ["What is the difference between a simple random sample and a convenience sample?","In a simple random sample everyone has an equal chance of being chosen. A convenience sample uses whoever is easiest to reach."],
   ["Name the three sources of information in a marketing information system.","Internal databases, marketing intelligence and marketing research."],
   ["What is a focus group?","A small group of about 6 to 10 people who talk about a product or issue with a trained moderator."]
  ],
  prompts:[
   "I am a Level 100 marketing student in the Maldives. Explain the difference between primary and secondary data in simple English. Give three Maldivian examples of each. Then give me five examples and ask me to sort them.",
   "Explain exploratory, descriptive and causal research in simple English, with one example each for a Maldivian business. Then describe two research problems and ask me which type fits each one.",
   "Act as my tutor. A small café in Hulhumalé wants to know why fewer students are coming in. Ask me to plan the research step by step: the problem, the data, the method and the sample. Give me feedback after each step."
  ]},

 /* ===================== TOPIC 5 ===================== */
 5:{sum:"What shapes consumer buying, the four types of buying behaviour, the buyer decision process, and business buyers.",
  min:["Buyers are influenced by four groups of factors: cultural, social, personal and psychological.","How much thought a buyer puts in depends on involvement and on how different the brands are. This gives four buying behaviours.","Most buyers pass through five stages: need recognition, information search, evaluation, purchase, and post-purchase behaviour.","Businesses buy differently from consumers: fewer, larger buyers, more people involved, and more formal steps."],
  lo:"Explain the factors that influence consumer and business buyers, and apply them to real buying decisions.",
  exam:["Section A: name the factor, behaviour type or stage in a short buying story.","Section B: explain a model of buying, or show how factors influence Maldivian buyers and how a business can use them."],
  ideas:[
   {h:"Model of consumer behaviour",p:"Consumer buyer behaviour is the buying behaviour of final consumers: people and households who buy for personal use. Marketers want to know how buyers respond to marketing. The textbook shows this as a stimulus-response model.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"Stimulus and response",steps:[["Marketing and other stimuli","The 4Ps, plus economic, technological, social and cultural forces."],["The buyer's black box","Buyer characteristics and the buyer decision process."],["Buyer responses","Attitudes and preferences, and what, when, where and how much they buy."]]},
    more:"The 'black box' is the buyer's mind. Marketers cannot see inside it, so they study the factors that shape it."},
   {h:"Cultural factors",p:"Cultural factors have the broadest and deepest influence.",
    l:[["Culture","The set of basic values, perceptions, wants and behaviours learned by a member of society from family and other important institutions. It is the most basic cause of a person's wants and behaviour."],["Subculture","A group of people with shared value systems based on common life experiences and situations, such as nationality, religion, racial group or region."],["Social class","Fairly permanent and ordered divisions in a society whose members share similar values, interests and behaviours. It is shown by a mix of occupation, income, education and wealth."]],
    eg:"Ramadan shapes what and when Maldivian families buy. Food shops stock dates and iftar items, and stay open late."},
   {h:"Social factors",l:[["Groups and social networks","Membership groups have a direct influence on a person. Reference groups are groups a person compares themselves with, or wants to belong to."],["Opinion leaders","People in a reference group who, because of special skills, knowledge or personality, influence others. Many brands now use influencers on social media."],["Family","The most important consumer buying group. Who decides depends on the product and the family."],["Roles and status","A role is what people expect a person to do. Each role carries status. People often buy products that show their status."]],
    eg:"A teenager buys the same trainers as a popular Maldivian footballer she follows. The footballer is an opinion leader."},
   {h:"Personal factors",p:"A buyer's own life and character also shape buying.",
    l:[["Age and life-cycle stage","Needs change as people grow older and as their family changes, from single, to married, to parent, to retired."],["Occupation","A person's job affects what they buy. A resort manager buys business clothes; a fisherman buys strong boots and boat parts."],["Economic situation","Income, savings and borrowing power affect how much people can spend."],["Lifestyle","A person's pattern of living, shown in their activities, interests and opinions (AIOs)."],["Personality and self-concept","Personality is the unique traits that shape lasting responses. Self-concept is how people see themselves. People often buy brands that match their self-image."]],
    eg:"A young couple with a baby now buys nappies and a car seat. Their life-cycle stage has changed."},
   {h:"Psychological factors",l:[["Motivation","A motive (or drive) is a need strong enough to make a person act. Maslow arranged needs in a hierarchy (see the pyramid)."],["Perception","How people select, organise and interpret information to form a meaningful picture of the world. Three processes: selective attention (screening out most messages), selective distortion (twisting information to fit what we believe) and selective retention (remembering what supports our beliefs)."],["Learning","Changes in a person's behaviour that come from experience."],["Beliefs and attitudes","A belief is a descriptive thought a person has about something. An attitude is a fairly consistent evaluation, feeling and tendency towards an object or idea. Attitudes are hard to change, so firms try to fit their products into existing attitudes."]],
    dia:{type:"pyramid",title:"Maslow's hierarchy of needs",levels:[["Self-actualisation","Self-development and realisation"],["Esteem","Self-esteem, recognition, status"],["Social","Belonging, love"],["Safety","Security, protection"],["Physiological","Hunger, thirst"]],note:"People try to meet the most basic needs (bottom) first, then move up."}},
   {h:"Four types of buying behaviour",p:"Buying behaviour depends on two things: how involved the buyer is, and how big the differences between brands are.",
    dia:{type:"grid",title:"Types of buying behaviour",yl:"Differences between brands",xl:"Buyer involvement",y:["Significant","Few"],x:["High","Low"],cells:[["Complex","Expensive, risky, rarely bought. The buyer learns a lot first. Example: a laptop."],["Variety-seeking","The buyer switches brands for variety. Example: biscuits."],["Dissonance-reducing","Expensive but brands look alike. Doubts may follow. Example: a sofa."],["Habitual","Little thought, same brand from habit. Example: salt."]]},
    tip:"First ask: is it expensive or risky (high involvement)? Then ask: are the brands very different? The two answers point to one box."},
   {h:"The buyer decision process",p:"Buyers usually pass through five stages. The process starts long before the purchase and continues long after it. For routine purchases, buyers may skip some stages.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"The buyer decision process",steps:[["Need recognition","The buyer sees a problem or need."],["Information search","Personal, commercial, public and experience sources."],["Evaluation of alternatives","Compares brands on what matters."],["Purchase decision","Chooses and buys the preferred brand."],["Post-purchase behaviour","Satisfied or not; may feel doubt."]]},
    l:[["Need recognition","Triggered by inside needs (hunger) or outside stimuli (an advert, a friend's new phone)."],["Information search","From personal sources (family, friends), commercial sources (ads, websites, sales staff), public sources (reviews, social media) and experience (trying it)."],["Evaluation of alternatives","The buyer compares brands on the attributes that matter most, such as price, quality and battery life."],["Purchase decision","Usually the preferred brand, but the attitudes of others and unexpected events (a price rise, losing a job) can change it."],["Post-purchase behaviour","The buyer compares performance with expectations. Big purchases often bring cognitive dissonance: discomfort caused by doubts after buying."]],
    eg:"Aisha's phone breaks (need recognition). She asks friends and watches YouTube reviews (search). She compares three models (evaluation). She buys one in Majeedhee Magu (purchase). She keeps checking reviews to feel sure (post-purchase)."},
   {h:"Business buyer behaviour",p:["Business buyer behaviour is the buying behaviour of organisations that buy goods and services to use in producing other products and services, or to resell them.","Business markets differ from consumer markets: they have fewer but larger buyers, demand is derived from consumer demand, purchases involve more people and a more professional effort, and buyers and sellers work more closely."],
    l:[["Straight rebuy","The buyer reorders something without any changes."],["Modified rebuy","The buyer wants to change the product, prices, terms or supplier."],["New task","The buyer buys something for the first time. Most people involved and most information gathered."],["Buying centre","All the people and units who take part in the business buying decision: users, influencers, buyers, deciders and gatekeepers."]],
    eg:"A resort buying its first desalination plant is a new task. The engineer, finance manager and general manager are all part of the buying centre."}
  ],
  terms:[["Culture","Basic values, perceptions, wants and behaviours learned from family and society."],["Subculture","A group with shared values from common life experiences."],["Social class","Lasting, ordered divisions in society with shared values and behaviours."],["Opinion leader","A person whose skills, knowledge or personality influence others."],["Lifestyle","A person's pattern of living: activities, interests and opinions."],["Motive","A need strong enough to make a person act."],["Perception","How people select, organise and interpret information."],["Selective attention","Screening out most of the information we see."],["Learning","Changes in behaviour that come from experience."],["Attitude","A fairly consistent evaluation and feeling towards an object or idea."],["Complex buying behaviour","High involvement and big brand differences."],["Habitual buying behaviour","Low involvement and few brand differences."],["Cognitive dissonance","Discomfort caused by doubts after buying."],["Derived demand","Business demand that comes from consumer demand."],["Buying centre","Everyone involved in a business buying decision."],["New task","A business buying something for the first time."]],
  mistakes:["Mixing up social factors (other people) and personal factors (the buyer's own life).","Putting the five buyer stages in the wrong order. Search comes before evaluation.","Forgetting post-purchase behaviour. The process does not end at the purchase.","Mixing up complex (big brand differences) and dissonance-reducing (few differences) buying.","Listing Maslow's levels without saying that people meet lower needs first."],
  worked:{q:"Explain how TWO psychological factors could influence a young person in Malé who is choosing a new smartphone. Suggest how a phone brand could use each factor.",
   plan:["Choose two psychological factors, such as perception and attitudes, or motivation and learning.","For each: define it in one sentence.","Explain how it affects the young buyer.","Suggest one action for the phone brand."],
   model:[["Define","Perception is how people select, organise and interpret information to form a picture of the world."],["Apply","Young people see hundreds of phone adverts, so selective attention means they ignore most of them. A phone brand should use eye-catching short videos made by local creators, so its message gets noticed."],["Define","An attitude is a person's fairly consistent evaluation and feeling towards an object or idea."],["Apply","If young buyers believe one brand is 'cool' and another is 'for older people', that attitude is hard to change. A brand with a weaker image should fit into existing attitudes, for example by stressing the camera quality and gaming features that young people already value."],["Link","By understanding perception and attitudes, the brand can shape its message so young buyers notice it and feel good about it."]]},
  check:[
   ["What is the most basic cause of a person's wants and behaviour?","Culture."],
   ["A shopper buys whatever brand of sugar is on the shelf without thinking. Which buying behaviour is this?","Habitual buying behaviour."],
   ["Name the five stages of the buyer decision process in order.","Need recognition, information search, evaluation of alternatives, purchase decision, post-purchase behaviour."],
   ["Which Maslow need comes straight after physiological needs?","Safety needs."],
   ["What is derived demand?","Business demand that comes from the demand for consumer goods."]
  ],
  prompts:[
   "I am a Level 100 marketing student in the Maldives. Explain the cultural, social, personal and psychological factors that affect consumer buying, in simple English. Give one Maldivian example for each group. Then quiz me with four questions, one at a time.",
   "Explain the four types of buying behaviour (complex, dissonance-reducing, habitual and variety-seeking) in simple English. Use a 2 by 2 table. Then give me six Maldivian products and ask me to place each one.",
   "Act as my tutor. Tell me a short story about a Maldivian student buying a new phone. Stop at each stage of the buyer decision process and ask me which stage it is and what a phone shop could do at that stage."
  ]},

 /* ===================== TOPIC 6 ===================== */
 6:{sum:"Segmentation, targeting, differentiation and positioning: choosing customers and a clear place in their minds.",
  min:["A company cannot serve everyone well, so it chooses its customers.","Segmentation splits the market into groups. Targeting chooses which groups to serve.","Differentiation makes the offer different and better. Positioning gives it a clear place in customers' minds.","A positioning statement and a value proposition sum up why customers should buy."],
  lo:"Apply market segmentation, targeting, differentiation and positioning to design a customer value-driven strategy.",
  exam:["Section A: name the segmentation base, targeting strategy or value proposition in a short story.","Section B: explain the steps, bases or requirements, or recommend a target market and position for a Maldivian business."],
  ideas:[
   {h:"Four steps of a customer value-driven strategy",p:"The first two steps choose the customers to serve. The last two steps decide how to create value for them.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"Customer value-driven marketing strategy",steps:[["Segmentation","Divide the whole market into smaller groups."],["Targeting","Choose the segment or segments to serve."],["Differentiation","Make the offer different to create superior value."],["Positioning","Give it a clear, desirable place in customers' minds."]]},
    l:[["Market segmentation","Dividing a market into smaller segments of buyers with different needs, characteristics or behaviours, who might need separate marketing strategies or mixes."],["Market targeting","Evaluating each segment's attractiveness and selecting one or more segments to serve."],["Differentiation","Actually differentiating the market offering to create superior customer value."],["Positioning","Arranging for a market offering to occupy a clear, distinctive and desirable place relative to competing products in the minds of target consumers."]],
    tip:"Remember the order with 'S, T, D, P': first who, then how."},
   {h:"Bases for segmenting consumer markets",p:"There is no single way to segment a market. Marketers try different variables, alone or together.",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Four bases for segmenting consumers",cols:[["Geographic",["Countries, regions, atolls, islands, cities","Climate, city or island living"]],["Demographic",["Age and life-cycle stage","Gender, income, occupation, education, religion"]],["Psychographic",["Lifestyle","Personality","Social class"]],["Behavioural",["Occasions","Benefits sought","User status and usage rate","Loyalty status"]]]},
    l:[["Occasion segmentation","Grouping buyers by when they get the idea to buy, buy or use the product, such as Eid, weddings or school holidays."],["Benefit segmentation","Grouping buyers by the benefits they want, such as low price, speed or luxury."]],
    eg:"A Maldivian airline could segment by country of origin (geographic), income (demographic), adventure lovers (psychographic) and how often people fly (behavioural)."},
   {h:"Requirements for effective segmentation",p:"Not every way of splitting a market is useful. To be useful, a segment must be:",
    l:[["Measurable","Its size, buying power and profile can be measured."],["Accessible","It can be reached and served well."],["Substantial","It is large or profitable enough to be worth serving."],["Differentiable","It responds differently to marketing than other segments."],["Actionable","The company can design programmes to attract and serve it."]],
    tip:"Memory aid: MASDA. Measurable, Accessible, Substantial, Differentiable, Actionable."},
   {h:"Evaluating and selecting segments",p:["To evaluate segments, a company looks at three things: the segment's size and growth, its structural attractiveness (competitors, substitutes, the power of buyers and suppliers), and whether it fits the company's objectives and resources.","A target market is a set of buyers who share common needs or characteristics that the company decides to serve."]},
   {h:"Targeting strategies",p:"Targeting can be very broad or very narrow.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"From broad to narrow targeting",steps:[["Undifferentiated (mass)","One offer for the whole market."],["Differentiated (segmented)","Separate offers for several segments."],["Concentrated (niche)","A large share of one or a few small segments."],["Micromarketing","Local marketing or individual (one-to-one) marketing."]]},
    l:[["Choosing a strategy","Consider the company's resources (few resources suit concentrated marketing), how variable the product is, the product's life-cycle stage, how different buyers are, and what competitors do."],["Socially responsible targeting","Avoid unfair targeting of vulnerable groups, such as children, with harmful products."]],
    eg:"A resort group with separate brands for honeymooners, families and divers uses differentiated marketing. A small dive shop that serves only technical divers is a niche marketer."},
   {h:"Differentiation and competitive advantage",p:"A competitive advantage is an advantage over competitors gained by offering greater customer value, either through lower prices or by giving more benefits that justify higher prices. A company can differentiate in five ways.",
    l:[["Product differentiation","Features, performance, style or design."],["Services differentiation","Speed, convenience, careful delivery, customer care."],["Channel differentiation","The way the product reaches customers: coverage, expertise and performance."],["People differentiation","Hiring and training better staff."],["Image differentiation","A brand image or symbol that carries a clear message."]],
    more:"A difference is worth promoting if it is important, distinctive, superior, communicable, pre-emptive (hard to copy), affordable and profitable.",
    eg:"A guesthouse with a resident marine biologist offers a service difference rivals cannot easily copy."},
   {h:"Positioning maps and value propositions",p:["A perceptual positioning map shows how consumers see brands compared with each other on important buying dimensions, such as price and quality.","A brand's value proposition is the full mix of benefits on which it is differentiated and positioned. It answers the customer's question: 'Why should I buy your brand?'"],
    dia:{type:"grid",title:"A simple positioning map for cafés in Malé",yl:"Price",xl:"Atmosphere",y:["High","Low"],x:["Premium","Basic"],cells:[["Hotel coffee lounge","Comfortable, stylish, expensive."],["(Gap)","High price with a basic feel. Few buyers want this."],["Mid-range study café","Fair price, quiet and comfortable. A possible gap."],["Tea shop (sai hotaa)","Cheap, quick, simple."]]},
    l:[["More for more","The best product at a higher price."],["More for the same","Similar quality, but more benefits, at the same price as rivals."],["The same for less","The same products at a lower price."],["Less for much less","Fewer benefits at a much lower price."],["More for less","More benefits at a lower price. Hard to keep up over time."]]},
   {h:"The positioning statement",p:"A positioning statement sums up the company or brand positioning in one sentence. It follows this form:",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"Positioning statement form",steps:[["To (target segment and need)","Who it is for and what they need."],["our (brand) is","The brand name."],["(concept)","The category it belongs to."],["that (point of difference)","What makes it different and better."]]},
    eg:"To busy office workers in Malé who want a quick healthy lunch, Fresh Bowl is the salad bar that serves your meal in under five minutes."}
  ],
  terms:[["Market segmentation","Dividing a market into smaller groups with different needs, characteristics or behaviours."],["Market targeting","Evaluating segments and selecting one or more to serve."],["Differentiation","Making the offer different to create superior customer value."],["Positioning","Giving the offer a clear, distinctive and desirable place in target customers' minds."],["Geographic segmentation","Dividing by place: countries, regions, islands."],["Demographic segmentation","Dividing by age, gender, income, occupation and so on."],["Psychographic segmentation","Dividing by lifestyle, personality or social class."],["Behavioural segmentation","Dividing by knowledge, attitudes, use or response to a product."],["Target market","Buyers with common needs the company decides to serve."],["Undifferentiated marketing","One offer for the whole market."],["Differentiated marketing","Separate offers for several segments."],["Concentrated marketing","A large share of one or a few small segments (niches)."],["Micromarketing","Tailoring to local groups or individuals."],["Competitive advantage","An advantage over rivals from greater customer value."],["Value proposition","The full mix of benefits a brand is positioned on."],["Positioning statement","To (target) our (brand) is (concept) that (point of difference)."]],
  mistakes:["Mixing up segmentation (dividing) and targeting (choosing).","Calling lifestyle 'demographic'. Lifestyle is psychographic.","Calling usage rate or loyalty 'psychographic'. They are behavioural.","Writing a positioning statement without a point of difference.","Mixing up differentiation (making the offer different) and positioning (the place in the customer's mind)."],
  worked:{q:"Explain the four main targeting strategies. For each one, give an example of a Maldivian business that might use it.",
   plan:["One sentence on what targeting is.","Take the four strategies from broad to narrow.","For each: one sentence on what it is and one Maldivian example.","Keep the examples different from each other."],
   model:[["Define","Market targeting means evaluating each segment's attractiveness and choosing one or more segments to serve. Strategies range from very broad to very narrow."],["Explain","Undifferentiated (mass) marketing ignores segment differences and offers one product to the whole market. A basic bottled water brand sold the same way everywhere could use it."],["Explain","Differentiated marketing targets several segments with a separate offer for each. A resort group with separate resorts for families, honeymooners and divers uses it."],["Explain","Concentrated (niche) marketing goes after a large share of one or a few small segments. A small liveaboard company that serves only experienced shark divers uses it."],["Explain","Micromarketing tailors offers to local groups or individuals. A supermarket chain that stocks fishing gear in its Addu branch and office snacks in Malé uses local marketing."]]},
  check:[
   ["What is the difference between segmentation and targeting?","Segmentation divides the market into groups. Targeting chooses which of those groups to serve."],
   ["A phone shop groups customers by how often they upgrade their phone. Which base is this?","Behavioural segmentation (usage rate)."],
   ["Name the five requirements for useful segments.","Measurable, accessible, substantial, differentiable and actionable."],
   ["A resort offers private butlers at a very high price. Which value proposition is this?","More for more."],
   ["Which targeting strategy suits a small company with few resources?","Concentrated (niche) marketing."]
  ],
  prompts:[
   "I am a Level 100 marketing student in the Maldives. Explain the four bases for segmenting consumer markets in simple English. For a local airline, give one segment for each base. Then ask me to segment a different Maldivian business.",
   "Explain the four targeting strategies (undifferentiated, differentiated, concentrated and micromarketing) in simple English with Maldivian examples. Then describe three businesses and ask me which strategy each one uses.",
   "Act as my tutor. Help me write a positioning statement for a new guesthouse on a local island. Ask me questions about the target customers and the point of difference, one at a time. Then check my final statement against the textbook form."
  ]},

 /* ===================== TOPIC 7 ===================== */
 7:{sum:"Levels of product, types of products, product and brand decisions, and the special nature of services.",
  min:["A product is anything offered to satisfy a need or want. It has three levels: core value, actual product and augmented product.","Consumer products are convenience, shopping, specialty or unsought. They are marketed differently.","Services are intangible, inseparable, variable and perishable, so they need special marketing.","Brands add value. Companies choose how to position, name, sponsor and develop their brands."],
  lo:"Explain product, service and brand decisions, and apply them to products and services in the Maldives.",
  exam:["Section A: classify products, name service characteristics, and know product mix and brand terms.","Section B: explain a product or service idea, or apply product and brand decisions to a Maldivian business."],
  ideas:[
   {h:"Products, services and experiences",p:["A product is anything that can be offered to a market for attention, acquisition, use or consumption that might satisfy a want or need. It includes physical objects, services, events, persons, places, organisations and ideas.","A service is an activity, benefit or satisfaction offered for sale that is essentially intangible and does not result in the ownership of anything.","Many companies now create customer experiences: memorable moments around the product."],
    eg:"A resort does not just rent rooms. It sells sunset dolphin trips, island dinners and memories."},
   {h:"Three levels of product",p:"Each level adds more customer value. Planners start in the middle and work outward.",
    dia:{type:"rings",title:"Three levels of product",rings:[["Augmented product","Extra services and benefits: delivery, warranty, after-sale support, credit."],["Actual product","Features, design, quality level, brand name and packaging."],["Core customer value","The real benefit or problem-solving service the buyer is seeking."]]},
    l:[["Core customer value","Ask: what is the buyer really buying? A person buying a phone is buying connection, convenience and status."],["Actual product","The product people can see and use: its parts, quality, style, brand name and packaging."],["Augmented product","The extra services and benefits that build around the core and actual product."]],
    eg:"A mobile data plan sells staying connected with family (core), a 30 GB bundle with a brand name and an app (actual), and free customer support around the clock (augmented)."},
   {h:"Consumer products",p:"Consumer products are bought by final consumers for personal use. They are grouped by how consumers buy them, and each type needs different marketing.",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Four types of consumer products",cols:[["Convenience",["Bought often, quickly, with little effort","Low price, sold everywhere","Bread, rice, phone top-ups"]],["Shopping",["Bought less often; buyers compare quality, price and style","Fewer outlets, more sales help","Phones, clothes, furniture"]],["Specialty",["Unique features or brand; buyers make a special effort","High price, few outlets","Luxury watches, a famous restaurant"]],["Unsought",["Not known, or not normally thought of","Needs much advertising and personal selling","Life insurance, funeral services"]]]}},
   {h:"Industrial products",p:"Industrial products are bought by people and organisations for further processing or for use in running a business. The difference depends on why the product is bought, not on the product itself.",
    l:[["Materials and parts","Raw materials and manufactured parts, such as fish for a canning factory."],["Capital items","Products that help in production or operations, such as boats, generators and machines."],["Supplies and services","Operating supplies, repair and maintenance items, and business services such as legal advice."]]},
   {h:"Individual product and service decisions",p:"Marketers make five main decisions about each product.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"Individual product decisions",steps:[["Attributes","Quality, features, style and design."],["Branding","Name, sign or symbol."],["Packaging","The container or wrapper."],["Labelling","Identifies, describes, informs."],["Support services","Help that adds value."]]},
    l:[["Product and service attributes","Product quality (performance and consistency), product features, and product style and design."],["Branding","A brand is a name, term, sign, symbol or design, or a mix of these, that identifies the maker or seller and makes the product different from competitors."],["Packaging","Designing and producing the container or wrapper. It protects the product and also attracts buyers and tells the brand's story."],["Labelling","Identifies the product or brand, describes it (who made it, where, when, what is inside), and may promote it. Labels may also carry legal information, such as nutrition facts or a halal mark."],["Product support services","Services that add to the actual product, such as delivery, repairs and a customer hotline."]]},
   {h:"Product line and product mix",p:"A product line is a group of products that are closely related because they work in a similar way, are sold to the same customers, use the same outlets, or fall within a price range. The product mix (or portfolio) is all the product lines and items a company sells.",
    dia:{type:"compare",title:"Product mix of an imaginary Maldivian food company",cols:[["Line 1: Canned tuna",["In oil","In water","Chilli","Each in 2 sizes"]],["Line 2: Snacks",["Tuna chips","Coconut biscuits"]],["Line 3: Drinks",["Coconut water","Lime juice"]]]},
    l:[["Width","The number of product lines. Here: 3."],["Length","The total number of items in all the lines. Here: 7."],["Depth","The number of versions of each product. Each tuna has 2 sizes."],["Consistency","How closely the lines are related in use, production and distribution. Here: fairly consistent, all food sold in shops."]]},
   {h:"Four characteristics of services",p:"Services differ from physical goods in four ways. Each one creates a challenge, and each has a way to manage it.",
    l:[["Intangibility","Services cannot be seen, tasted, felt, heard or smelled before they are bought. To manage it: give signs of quality, such as photos, reviews, clean premises and smart uniforms."],["Inseparability","Services are produced and consumed at the same time, and cannot be separated from their providers. The customer is part of the service. To manage it: train staff well, because they are part of the product."],["Variability","Quality depends on who provides the service, and when, where and how. To manage it: hire carefully, train staff, set clear standards and watch customer satisfaction."],["Perishability","Services cannot be stored for later sale or use. To manage it: match supply and demand with off-peak prices, early booking offers and reservations."]],
    tip:"Memory aid: 'I I V P', or 'In Its Very Place'. For full marks, give the meaning, an example and how to manage it."},
   {h:"Marketing strategies for service firms",p:"Because staff and customers meet face to face, service firms must look after both.",
    dia:{type:"flow",title:"The service profit chain",steps:[["Internal service quality","Good training, support and working conditions."],["Satisfied, productive staff","Happy, loyal, hard-working employees."],["Greater service value","Better, more efficient service."],["Satisfied, loyal customers","They return and recommend."],["Healthy profits and growth","Better results for the firm."]]},
    l:[["Internal marketing","Training and motivating customer-contact staff and support staff to work as a team to give customer satisfaction. It must come before external marketing."],["Interactive marketing","Training staff in the skill of serving customers, because service quality depends on the quality of the buyer-seller interaction."],["Managing differentiation, quality and productivity","Service firms must also stand out from rivals, deliver consistently high quality, and work efficiently without cutting quality."]]},
   {h:"Brands and brand equity",p:["Brands are a company's most lasting assets. Consumers see a brand as an important part of a product.","Brand equity is the differential effect that knowing the brand name has on customer response to the product or its marketing. A strong brand has high brand equity: customers prefer it, pay more for it and stay loyal."],
    eg:"Customers may choose one telecom company over another at the same price because they trust the brand more. That extra pull is brand equity."},
   {h:"Building strong brands: four decisions",l:[["Brand positioning","On attributes (what it has), on benefits (what it does for me), or on beliefs and values (how it makes me feel). Values are the strongest."],["Brand name selection","Easy to say and remember, suggests benefits, distinctive, and can be legally protected."],["Brand sponsorship","National (manufacturer) brand, store (private) brand, licensing (paying to use another's name or character), or co-branding (two established brands on one product)."],["Brand development","Line extensions, brand extensions, multibrands or new brands (see the grid)."]],
    dia:{type:"grid",title:"Brand development strategies",yl:"Brand name",xl:"Product category",y:["Existing","New"],x:["Existing","New"],cells:[["Line extension","Same brand, new flavour, size or form in the same category."],["Brand extension","Same brand moved into a new category."],["Multibrands","New brand names in the same category."],["New brands","New brand names in a new category."]]},
    eg:"A tuna brand that adds a chilli flavour uses a line extension. If it launches a tuna-flavoured snack under the same name, that is a brand extension."}
  ],
  terms:[["Product","Anything offered to a market that might satisfy a want or need."],["Service","An intangible activity or benefit that does not result in ownership."],["Core customer value","The real benefit the buyer is seeking."],["Augmented product","Extra services and benefits around the actual product."],["Convenience product","Bought often and quickly, with little effort."],["Shopping product","Compared carefully on quality, price and style."],["Specialty product","Unique; buyers make a special effort to get it."],["Unsought product","Not known, or not normally thought of buying."],["Product line","A group of closely related products."],["Product mix width","The number of product lines."],["Intangibility","Services cannot be sensed before purchase."],["Inseparability","Services cannot be separated from their providers."],["Variability","Service quality depends on who, when, where and how."],["Perishability","Services cannot be stored for later."],["Service profit chain","Links staff satisfaction, customer satisfaction and profit."],["Internal marketing","Training and motivating staff to satisfy customers."],["Brand equity","The extra effect the brand name has on customer response."],["Co-branding","Two established brand names on one product."],["Line extension","Existing brand, new form in the same category."]],
  mistakes:["Mixing up core value (the benefit) and the actual product (the features).","Calling a free extra service part of the actual product. Extras are augmented.","Mixing up variability (quality changes) and inseparability (cannot be separated from the provider).","Naming the service characteristics without saying how to manage them.","Mixing up line extensions (same category) and brand extensions (new category)."],
  worked:{q:"A large supermarket chain in Malé is deciding how to sponsor the brands on its shelves. Explain the four brand sponsorship options and suggest which ones the supermarket could use.",
   plan:["One sentence on what brand sponsorship means: whose name goes on the product.","Explain each of the four options in one or two sentences.","Recommend one or two for the supermarket, with a reason."],
   model:[["Define","Brand sponsorship is the decision about whose brand name a product carries."],["Explain","A national (manufacturer) brand is created and owned by the maker, such as a well-known international biscuit brand. A store (private) brand is created and owned by the reseller, here the supermarket itself."],["Explain","Licensing means paying to use names or characters created by others, such as cartoon characters on children's products. Co-branding means two established brands appear on the same product."],["Recommend","The supermarket should sell national brands, because shoppers trust them, and also launch its own store brand for basics such as rice, sugar and cooking oil."],["Reason","A store brand lets the supermarket offer lower prices than national brands while keeping a better profit margin, and it builds loyalty to the store itself."]]},
  check:[
   ["What is the difference between the actual product and the augmented product?","The actual product is the product itself: features, design, brand and packaging. The augmented product adds extra services and benefits, such as delivery or a warranty."],
   ["Guests get very different service depending on which staff member is on duty. Which service characteristic is this?","Variability."],
   ["How can a ferry company manage perishability?","By matching supply and demand, for example with lower off-peak fares, early booking discounts and reservations."],
   ["A tuna brand adds a new chilli flavour under its existing name. Which brand development strategy is this?","A line extension."],
   ["Into which group of consumer products does life insurance usually fall?","Unsought products."]
  ],
  prompts:[
   "I am a Level 100 marketing student in the Maldives. Explain the three levels of product in simple English. Show all three levels for a speedboat transfer service and for a café in Malé. Then ask me to do the same for a dive centre.",
   "Explain the four characteristics of services (intangibility, inseparability, variability and perishability) in simple English. For each one, give a Maldivian tourism example and one way a business can manage it. Then quiz me with four questions.",
   "Act as my tutor. Explain the four brand development strategies (line extension, brand extension, multibrands and new brands) using a Maldivian food brand. Then give me three new product ideas and ask me which strategy fits each one."
  ]}
 }
}
};
